1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,560
TUTOR: OK, so what I'd like you to do now is to talk to your partner about your presentations on urban planning.

2
00:00:09,560 --> 00:00:15,250
You should have done most of the reading now, so I'd like you to share your ideas,

3
00:00:15,250 --> 00:00:20,270
and talk about the structure of your presentation and what you need to do next.

4
00:00:20,270 --> 00:00:24,820
CARLA: OK Rob. I'm glad we chose quite a specific topic

5
00:00:25,200 --> 00:00:27,286
cities built next to the sea.

6
00:00:27,286 --> 00:00:31,000
It made it much easier to find relevant information.

7
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:33,856
ROB: Yeah. And cities are growing so quickly -

8
00:00:33,856 --> 00:00:39,210
I mean, we know that more than half the world's population lives in cities now.

9
00:00:39,210 --> 00:00:43,675
CARLA: Yeah, though that's all cities, not just ones on the coast.

10
00:00:43,675 --> 00:00:47,780
But most of the biggest cities are actually built by the sea

11
00:00:48,020 --> 00:00:50,000
I'd not realised that before.

12
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:53,000
ROB: Nor me. And what's more

13
00:00:53,060 --> 00:00:56,750
a lot of them are built at places where rivers come out into the sea.

14
00:00:56,750 --> 00:00:59,000
But apparently this can be a problem.

15
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:00,390
CARLA: Why?

16
00:01:00,390 --> 00:01:02,860
ROB: Well, as the city expands

17
00:01:03,440 --> 00:01:07,524
agriculture and industry tend to spread further inland along the rivers,

18
00:01:07,524 --> 00:01:11,720
and so agriculture moves even further inland up the river.

19
00:01:11,720 --> 00:01:14,180
That's not necessarily a problem

20
00:01:14,300 --> 00:01:18,610
except it means more and more pollutants are discharged into the rivers.

21
00:01:18,610 --> 00:01:22,880
CARLA: So these are brought downstream to the cities?

22
00:01:22,880 --> 00:01:26,720
ROB: Right. Hmm. Did you read that article about Miami

23
00:01:26,720 --> 00:01:29,110
on the east coast of the USA?

24
00:01:29,110 --> 00:01:30,570
CARLA: No.

25
00:01:30,570 --> 00:01:37,530
ROB: Well, apparently back in the 1950s they built channels to drain away the water in case of flooding.

26
00:01:37,530 --> 00:01:39,560
CARLA: Sounds sensible.

27
00:01:39,560 --> 00:01:42,940
ROB: Yeah, they spent quite a lot of money on them

28
00:01:42,940 --> 00:01:46,950
But what they didn't take into account was global warming.

29
00:01:46,950 --> 00:01:50,750
So they built the drainage channels too close to sea level,

30
00:01:50,750 --> 00:01:54,550
and now sea levels are rising, they're more or less useless.

31
00:01:54,550 --> 00:01:58,220
If there's a lot of rain, the water can't run away

32
00:01:58,380 --> 00:02:02,390
there's nowhere for it to go. The whole design was faulty.

33
00:02:02,390 --> 00:02:07,120
CARLA: So what are the authorities doing about it now?

34
00:02:07,120 --> 00:02:15,300
ROB: I don't know. I did read that they're aiming to stop disposing of waste water into the ocean over the next ten years.

35
00:02:15,300 --> 00:02:20,160
CARLA: But that won't help with flood prevention now, will it?

36
00:02:20,160 --> 00:02:28,873
ROB: No. Really they just need to find the money for something to replace the drainage channels in order to protect against flooding now.

37
00:02:28,873 --> 00:02:33,230
But in the long term they need to consider the whole ecosystem.

38
00:02:33,230 --> 00:02:40,826
CARLA: Right. Really, though, coastal cities can't deal with their problems on their own, can they? I mean,

39
00:02:40,826 --> 00:02:48,000
they've got to start acting together at an international level instead of just doing their own thing.

40
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:52,091
ROB: Absolutely. The thing is, everyone knows what the problems are

41
00:02:52,091 --> 00:02:57,669
and environmentalists have a pretty good idea of what we should be doing about them,

42
00:02:57,669 --> 00:03:01,760
so they should be able to work together to some extent.

43
00:03:01,760 --> 00:03:09,260
But it's going to be a long time before countries come to a decision on what principles they're prepared to abide by.

44
00:03:09,260 --> 00:03:12,550
CARLA: Yes, if they ever do.

45
00:03:12,550 --> 00:03:19,250
CARLA: So I think we've probably got enough for our presentation. It's only fifteen minutes.

46
00:03:19,250 --> 00:03:22,571
ROB: OK. So I suppose we'll begin

47
00:03:22,571 --> 00:03:27,790
with some general historical background about why coastal cities were established.

48
00:03:27,790 --> 00:03:31,149
But we don't want to spend too long on that.

49
00:03:31,149 --> 00:03:33,500
The other students will already know a bit about it

50
00:03:34,180 --> 00:03:37,530
It's all to do with communications and so on.

51
00:03:37,530 --> 00:03:42,580
CARLA: Yes. We should mention some geographical factors

52
00:03:43,060 --> 00:03:44,513
things like wetlands and river estuaries

53
00:03:44,513 --> 00:03:48,080
and coastal erosion and so on

54
00:03:48,460 --> 00:03:53,490
We could have some maps of different cities with these features marked.

55
00:03:53,490 --> 00:03:59,090
ROB: On a handout you mean? Or some slides everyone can see?

56
00:03:59,090 --> 00:04:02,580
CARLA: Yeah, that'd be better.

57
00:04:02,580 --> 00:04:07,146
ROB: It'd be good to go into past mistakes in a bit more detail.

58
00:04:07,146 --> 00:04:13,670
Did you read that case study of the problems there were in New Orleans with flooding a few years ago?

59
00:04:13,670 --> 00:04:18,954
CARLA: Yes. We could use that as the basis for that part of the talk.

60
00:04:18,954 --> 00:04:22,477
I don't think the other students will have read it,

61
00:04:22,477 --> 00:04:26,000
but they'll remember hearing about the flooding at the time.

62
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:32,000
ROB: OK. So that's probably enough background.

63
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:40,240
CARLA: So then we'll go on to talk about what action's being taken to deal with the problems of coastal cities.

64
00:04:40,240 --> 00:04:44,505
ROB: OK. What else do we need to talk about?

65
00:04:44,505 --> 00:04:46,860
Maybe something on future risks

66
00:04:47,020 --> 00:04:51,330
looking more at the long term, if populations continue to grow.

67
00:04:51,330 --> 00:04:55,140
CARLA: Yeah. We'll need to do a bit of work there

68
00:04:55,660 --> 00:04:59,460
I haven't got much information, have you?

69
00:04:59,460 --> 00:05:04,820
ROB: No. We'll need to look at some websites. Shouldn't take too long.

70
00:05:04,820 --> 00:05:10,423
CARLA: OK. And I think we should end by talking about international implications.

71
00:05:10,423 --> 00:05:14,300
Maybe we could ask people in the audience

72
00:05:14,820 --> 00:05:18,180
We've got people from quite a lot of different places.

73
00:05:18,180 --> 00:05:24,490
ROB: That'd be interesting, if we have time, yes. So now shall we...
